This paper examines a specific type of singular quasi-linear elliptic system, 
which is defined by the following equations:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{cases}
-\Delta_{p}u=a(x)|v|^{q}+b(x)|v|^{q}\ln(e+|v|),\quad x\in\Omega,\\
-\Delta_{p}v=a(x)|u|^{q}+b(x)|u|^{q}\ln(e+|u|),\quad x\in\Omega,\\
u=v=0,\quad x\in\partial\Omega,
\end{cases}
\end{equation*}
where $p,q>1$, $\Omega$ is a bounded domain in $\mathbb{R}^N$ with $N\geq 3$, and $a(x)$ and $b(x)$ are non-negative functions. The existence of solutions to this system is established using variational methods, specifically the Nehari manifold and fibering maps.
